:name: CubeRange constellation
:description: Two independent spacecraft, four nodes each, in one emulation

# Execution profile (design 4.3; rules G1/G2 of section 9.2), included FIRST so that a profile
# which cannot be found takes the machines down with it rather than leaving eight nodes running
# with no quantum set.
$profile?=@~/space-cs-sim/scripts/profiles/interactive.resc
include $profile

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- satellite 0
# CSP addresses OBC 1, EPS 2, ADCS 4, COMM 5. SCID 0x0A9. Space link on 3777.
# Every path below hangs off $out, and $out has NO default on purpose.
#
# These lines used to read @/tmp/cuberange/build-comm/zephyr/zephyr.elf and so on - one absolute
# path shared by every checkout of this repository. A second working copy on the same machine
# built into it, and this scenario then loaded THAT checkout's firmware without a word. The
# exercises made it worse by overriding only the one image under test, so the node being attacked
# came from here and the nodes around it came from somebody else.
#
# Unset, $out resolves to nothing and LoadELF fails where you can see it. That is the intended
# behaviour: refusing to start beats starting with the wrong firmware. The harness always passes
# it; for a manual run, so must you:
#
#   renode -e '$out=@'"$(make -s out)" -e 'include @<this file>' -e start
$comm0?=$out/build-comm/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$obc0?=$out/build-obc/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$eps0?=$out/build-eps-vuln/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$adcs0?=$out/build-adcs-vuln/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$comm0uart?=$out/sat0-comm.uart
$obc0uart?=$out/sat0-obc.uart
$eps0uart?=$out/sat0-eps.uart
$adcs0uart?=$out/sat0-adcs.uart
$link0?=3777

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- satellite 1
# CSP addresses OBC 9, EPS 10, ADCS 12, COMM 13. SCID 0x0AA. Space link on 3787.
$comm1?=$out/build-comm-sat1/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$obc1?=$out/build-obc-sat1/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$eps1?=$out/build-eps-sat1/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$adcs1?=$out/build-adcs-sat1/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$comm1uart?=$out/sat1-comm.uart
$obc1uart?=$out/sat1-obc.uart
$eps1uart?=$out/sat1-eps.uart
$adcs1uart?=$out/sat1-adcs.uart
$link1?=3787

# TWO hubs, and the names are what keeps them apart. Hub names are emulation-scope, so a scenario
# that reused "canHub" would silently join both spacecraft to one bus - and the symptom would be a
# range where an attack on one satellite mysteriously worked on the other. The test in
# tests/e2e/test_constellation.py asserts the isolation rather than assuming it.
emulation CreateCANHub "canHub0"
emulation CreateCANHub "canHub1"

mach create "SAT0_COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $comm0
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $comm0uart true
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $link0 "spacelink0" false
connector Connect sysbus.usart2 spacelink0
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT0_OBC"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $obc0
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $obc0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT0_EPS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $eps0
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $eps0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT0_ADCS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $adcs0
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $adcs0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT1_COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub1
sysbus LoadELF $comm1
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $comm1uart true
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $link1 "spacelink1" false
connector Connect sysbus.usart2 spacelink1
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT1_OBC"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub1
sysbus LoadELF $obc1
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $obc1uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT1_EPS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub1
sysbus LoadELF $eps1
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $eps1uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT1_ADCS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub1
sysbus LoadELF $adcs1
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $adcs1uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

# No attacker machine here. The constellation scenario exists to establish that two spacecraft can
# coexist and stay apart; the exercises that need an injector create their own on the hub they are
# attacking. Adding one to both hubs by default would put a raw CAN entry point on a spacecraft no
# exercise had asked to expose.
#
# ONE Monitor serves both, because rule L1 permits exactly one control client per emulation and
# both control listeners have a backlog of 1. Host code selects a spacecraft with `mach set`, which
# is why src/cuberange/renode/monitor.py serialises the session - a `mach set` from one satellite's
# poller landing inside another's read returns the wrong machine's register with no error.
